gkey NEWS -- history of user-visible changes.

* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0 (unreleased)

** New features

gkey-service presents a FIDO2 (CTAP 2.0) security key to the system
through UHID.  It implements authenticatorMakeCredential,
authenticatorGetAssertion, authenticatorGetNextAssertion,
authenticatorGetInfo and authenticatorReset over CTAPHID, with
keepalive and cancellation support.

Credential private keys are ECC P-256 keys generated inside the TPM
and bound to it.  The credential list is encrypted with a key sealed
to the TPM.  TPM commands use a salted, encrypted session.

The user authenticates in the desktop's polkit dialog before every
operation, or an external command given with --verify-command decides.

gkey-service and gkey are installed set-user-ID gkey, the only account
the udev rule lets use the TPM and /dev/uhid, and the owner of the
polkit actions.  They drop back to the user after opening the devices
and connecting to polkit, so programs running as the user cannot use
the TPM or create HID devices, while the authentication dialog names
the web site.  Users no longer join the tss group.

gkey-service now logs what it does by default, one line per event: its
start, each registration and sign-in with the user's decision, and a
warning with the reason whenever a client's request is refused, such as
ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk, which the TPM cannot support.  Read it with
"journalctl --user -u gkey.service".  --verbose adds debugging messages
and --quiet shows only errors.

SSH keys can be kept in the TPM: "ssh-keygen -t ecdsa-sk" works.  To
make it so, registrations carry a "packed" self attestation signed by
the new credential, and the credProtect extension is supported.  Ed25519
keys (ed25519-sk) are not, since TPMs do not offer Ed25519.

The authentication dialogs have a consistent plain-text layout, a
headline then labelled lines such as "Website" and "Account", that
reads the same in every agent.  Long host names keep their end.

Every command of gkey asks for authentication through polkit, in the
desktop's dialog or, on a terminal, through pkttyagent.

New program gkey lists, inspects and deletes credentials.

Both programs follow the GNU conventions for options, --help and
--version.  The build system provides the standard GNU targets and a
Texinfo manual.
